148
00:16:17,145 --> 00:16:17,345
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

149
00:16:17,425 --> 00:16:17,625
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

150
00:16:17,665 --> 00:16:19,166
[SPEAKER_05]: That's exactly what I was going to say.

151
00:16:19,206 --> 00:16:19,446
[SPEAKER_05]: So,

152
00:16:20,586 --> 00:16:35,877
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is, when you have the expectation that the realization, the release, the giving up, is supposed to be pleasant immediately, the suttas actually say quite the opposite.

153
00:16:35,897 --> 00:16:37,838
[SPEAKER_00]: Especially if you haven't been developed in virtue.

154
00:16:39,599 --> 00:16:42,582
[SPEAKER_00]: The Buddha even said like, that which the noble ones regard as pleasant,

155
00:16:43,660 --> 00:16:46,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Ordinary people regard it as a bliss, and vice versa.

156
00:16:46,622 --> 00:17:09,077
[SPEAKER_05]: Like the cessation of... the cessation of everything, like the cessation of your sense of self, cessation of appropriation, cessation of any sense of control that you might have over things, for somebody who's still dependent on that sense of control, and still actively exerts it, and asserts it through their actions,

157
00:17:11,019 --> 00:17:18,424
[SPEAKER_00]: This is going to be like jumping in a pool of cold water, just when you're warm.

158
00:17:18,944 --> 00:17:23,907
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's going to be the complete opposite direction of everything that you're used to.

159
00:17:25,188 --> 00:17:26,989
[SPEAKER_05]: But, from a certain sense, you realize

160
00:17:29,136 --> 00:17:40,284
[SPEAKER_05]: The burden, the burdenlessness of pronunciation, of giving things up, of non-craving, is something that you need to learn to value before it can be pleasant.

161
00:17:41,265 --> 00:17:48,750
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why some suttas would say that once you've gone forth, the difficult thing is to be content.

162
00:17:51,031 --> 00:18:00,215
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and the other would say, like, the neutral feeling, when you don't understand it, it's going to be unpleasant.

163
00:18:00,255 --> 00:18:01,935
[SPEAKER_00]: When you understand it, it's going to be peaceful.

164
00:18:02,636 --> 00:18:05,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So, neutral feeling is basically that direction of

165
00:18:08,505 --> 00:18:34,825
[SPEAKER_00]: Giving up of engagements, giving up of concerns, giving up of of involvements, but when the mind is still with the momentum of passion, aversion, and delusion, yeah, it's like when you break a car, it's like, yeah, it's there's that momentum and and it's it's probably, yeah, it's going to start trying to convince you to to keep going.

166
00:18:36,713 --> 00:18:38,574
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's when you need to hold it back.

167
00:18:38,594 --> 00:18:40,755
[SPEAKER_05]: This is, again, going back to the simile of the elephant.

168
00:18:41,316 --> 00:18:43,657
[SPEAKER_05]: That is the actual purpose of the force of the paṭṭhāna.

169
00:18:43,677 --> 00:18:48,339
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not for you to just... just ignore the whole thing and enter a different dimension.

170
00:18:49,060 --> 00:18:57,904
[SPEAKER_05]: It's when the mind is pressuring you to continue with that momentum of concerns and passions and involvements.

171
00:18:57,985 --> 00:18:59,425
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if it's not, it's out of the precepts.

172
00:18:59,926 --> 00:19:02,227
[SPEAKER_05]: You tie it down and you refuse to

173
00:19:04,377 --> 00:19:09,167
[SPEAKER_05]: Lose the context of the body, feelings, mind and phenomena.

174
00:19:09,668 --> 00:19:15,420
[SPEAKER_05]: So, these are ways for you to be able to endure the pressure, not ways for you to be able to replace it.

175
00:19:17,342 --> 00:19:27,825
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why the Buddha would say, yeah, he compared the four satipaṭṭhānas to like the post to which you would tie down the elephant until it calms down.

176
00:19:28,065 --> 00:19:40,349
[SPEAKER_00]: So, far from being an immediately pleasant endeavor, it's actually like an arduous training, which, in a way, he had to keep encouraging monks to be diligent and not

177
00:19:42,643 --> 00:19:54,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Not a relaxer effort, because if it were a matter of just, like, sunshine and vacation from day one, then you wouldn't need an encouragement to do it.

178
00:20:20,623 --> 00:20:34,249
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that same pain... like, it's not a... it's not a torturous pain, like when you're kind of... not the same pain as... as...

179
00:20:34,269 --> 00:20:38,310
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't know... getting your limbs chopped off or something.

180
00:20:39,511 --> 00:20:48,795
[SPEAKER_05]: It's... it's the pain of just having a certain pressure there and not giving in to it, no matter how much... how strongly it arises.

181
00:20:50,332 --> 00:21:03,705
[SPEAKER_00]: That same pressure, if, when you understand it rightly, is where the peace comes from, because you highlight the aspect of, well, this is not right.

182
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:25,581
[SPEAKER_05]: See, that's the... that's the... that's another thing, like the... as we were saying, why the Buddha wouldn't be teaching... he wouldn't start his training to some random person by saying, oh, nothing is... there's no self, everything is impermanence, by telling them the four noble truths first.

183
00:21:26,201 --> 00:21:31,524
[SPEAKER_05]: He would always begin by saying, first, the clansman hears the teaching and gains faith, he goes forth,

184
00:21:32,044 --> 00:21:37,128
[SPEAKER_05]: Having gone forth, he establishes himself in virtue, then the sense restraint, then all these other things.

185
00:21:37,709 --> 00:21:49,739
[SPEAKER_00]: And at, like, at the last step of his whole training, then he gets to understand the four noble truths, impermanence, the three characteristics, whichever way he wants to approach it, the five aggregates.

186
00:21:50,339 --> 00:21:53,442
[SPEAKER_05]: Because if he were to start with that,

187
00:21:54,748 --> 00:21:58,854
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, telling yourself, oh, this is not mine, as people usually do, or there is no self.

188
00:21:59,915 --> 00:22:02,398
[SPEAKER_05]: You're doing that because you want to get rid of the pressure.

189
00:22:02,478 --> 00:22:12,212
[SPEAKER_05]: In other words, because you're taking it as mine, because you still insist in controlling it, that's why you're telling yourself these things.

190
00:22:17,115 --> 00:22:26,279
[SPEAKER_04]: On the other hand, when you've actually developed yourself, the prerequisites, then, this is not mine, is your way of enduring the pressure.

191
00:22:27,239 --> 00:22:28,660
[SPEAKER_04]: Not your way of running away from it.

192
00:22:37,203 --> 00:22:38,304
[SPEAKER_00]: And that endurance is pleasant.

193
00:22:39,864 --> 00:22:41,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, like when

194
00:22:43,770 --> 00:22:56,277
[SPEAKER_00]: When you hear about something happening somewhere else, you could have been there, some catastrophe happening, you could have been there.

195
00:22:57,833 --> 00:22:59,214
[SPEAKER_00]: But you realize that you weren't.

196
00:23:00,535 --> 00:23:03,077
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, like, with the natural thing, you would feel the relief.

197
00:23:03,878 --> 00:23:05,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the same thing in here.

198
00:23:05,319 --> 00:23:10,103
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, everything that happens internally, quote-unquote, you're not there with it.

199
00:23:10,243 --> 00:23:20,872
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's like hearing about floods and disasters, but then perpetually realizing that, you know, it's... you're elsewhere.

200
00:23:22,433 --> 00:23:23,954
[SPEAKER_05]: Now, it doesn't mean that you're unaware of it.

201
00:23:24,414 --> 00:23:29,156
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't mean that, like, I don't mean that you disappear into a different dimension.

202
00:23:31,057 --> 00:23:33,538
[SPEAKER_05]: Obviously, the five aggregates are still there and there's no outside of it.

203
00:23:34,579 --> 00:23:47,665
[SPEAKER_05]: But, eh... that sense of personal connection, like, as the Buddha would say, whatever feeling he feels, whether pleasant, unpleasant or neutral, he feels it detached or disconnected.

204
00:23:50,413 --> 00:23:53,897
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not that you don't feel it, it's that it's felt as disconnected.

205
00:24:05,870 --> 00:24:07,332
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, it doesn't bother you.

206
00:24:07,752 --> 00:24:07,973
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

207
00:24:08,814 --> 00:24:10,015
[SPEAKER_04]: But, see, again, you can...

208
00:24:11,375 --> 00:24:21,924
[SPEAKER_04]: Take that on an abstract level of a view before you have developed yourself, and still, your conception of like, oh, it doesn't bother me, would still imply some degree of management.

209
00:24:22,544 --> 00:24:37,296
[SPEAKER_05]: In order to really understand for yourself what it means not to be bothered, you have to develop the mind first, and then see, like, on that proper level of pressure being arisen, and not being moved, not as a matter of choice, because

210
00:24:38,217 --> 00:24:47,770
[SPEAKER_04]: For as long as there's pressure, like the pressure is affecting you, and it's moving you, your choice to try not to be moved would be a way of being moved.

211
00:24:48,150 --> 00:24:50,494
[SPEAKER_05]: There's no way you can escape that.

212
00:24:51,074 --> 00:24:52,376
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, as a matter of choice.

213
00:24:53,257 --> 00:24:54,339
[SPEAKER_05]: Only by developing

214
00:24:55,400 --> 00:25:07,947
[SPEAKER_05]: As the suttas would say, the aggregate of virtue, the proper behavior beforehand, to the point where it doesn't even take effort for you to maintain, and it's there on its own, as the aggregates are.

215
00:25:09,208 --> 00:25:20,434
[SPEAKER_05]: Then, it's like, you would be able to see what it is, see what it means, not to be affected on the right level, without you having to try not to be affected.

216
00:25:20,454 --> 00:25:21,635
[SPEAKER_05]: You recognize, oh, actually,

217
00:25:22,555 --> 00:25:37,962
[SPEAKER_05]: As long as I don't cross this line, which actually takes effort, but I didn't see it because I was used to going over that line, as a beginningless habit, if I don't cross that line, there's nothing that can affect me.

218
00:25:38,182 --> 00:25:41,503
[SPEAKER_03]: You don't even see that management as actually bothersome.

219
00:25:41,684 --> 00:25:42,524
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

220
00:25:42,544 --> 00:25:43,484
[SPEAKER_03]: Actually, it's a load.

221
00:25:47,586 --> 00:25:51,509
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, like, I think I've said it before, it's like Stockholm Syndrome.

222
00:25:51,789 --> 00:26:05,018
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like something that, you know, it's actually painful, but because you're so used to it, even when you get the chance to free yourself from it, which is like perpetually there,

223
00:26:05,918 --> 00:26:12,102
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't want it, because it feels more comfortable to keep doing what you're used to.

224
00:26:12,342 --> 00:26:25,151
[SPEAKER_05]: The immediate safety of just gratifying that desire for pleasure, or gratifying that aversion against pain, trying to get rid of it, that feels kind of...

225
00:26:28,025 --> 00:26:32,207
[SPEAKER_00]: It feels like a guaranteed, immediate reward.

226
00:26:32,627 --> 00:26:41,210
[SPEAKER_05]: Whereas, this is like... you're kind of open to full uncertainty.

227
00:26:41,370 --> 00:26:43,731
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, I can't...

228
00:26:44,491 --> 00:26:47,612
[SPEAKER_05]: I'm not going to control this anymore.

229
00:26:48,892 --> 00:26:50,693
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's a completely different type of

230
00:26:52,645 --> 00:26:57,029
[SPEAKER_05]: There's no ease or peace.

231
00:26:58,170 --> 00:27:15,846
[SPEAKER_05]: You can compare the peace of having your house or whatever always in the perfect temperature, the perfect humidity level, no wind, no insects, whatever, running water.

232
00:27:17,207 --> 00:27:24,311
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you know, all of that is perpetually subject to all sorts of things that can undermine it.

233
00:27:25,032 --> 00:27:31,375
[SPEAKER_05]: You know, the price of things, the water system breaks down, you have to repair it.

234
00:27:32,916 --> 00:27:41,521
[SPEAKER_05]: And now, compare that with the peace of living on the streets, not caring about whether it's hot, cold, um...

235
00:27:42,833 --> 00:27:48,017
[SPEAKER_05]: It's warm, humid, dry.

236
00:27:48,937 --> 00:27:58,604
[SPEAKER_05]: And you realize, oh, that's... there's basically, even from that mundane perspective, that other type of peace is much more difficult to take away.

237
00:27:59,545 --> 00:28:02,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Because literally, like, it basically doesn't depend on anything.

238
00:28:03,507 --> 00:28:06,810
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's the same with your own mind.

239
00:28:07,510 --> 00:28:17,598
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, the wrong type of peace is when you depend on your own aggregates, on your own senses, on your own internal situation being a certain way.

240
00:28:19,079 --> 00:28:26,685
[SPEAKER_05]: And everything, again, even the right things that the Buddha taught, like not-self and so on,

241
00:28:29,029 --> 00:28:37,754
[SPEAKER_05]: If you're not devout in virtue, you will be using them still to manage that internal environment, to keep the house to the perfect temperature that you prefer.

242
00:28:38,855 --> 00:28:50,502
[SPEAKER_05]: Whereas, it has to come first through you giving up that comfort of management to begin with, and literally going out into the homeless life, internally at least.

243
00:28:50,862 --> 00:28:57,186
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, you know, not trying to manage your own internal home, quote-unquote.

244
00:28:58,348 --> 00:29:02,174
[SPEAKER_04]: Because for as long as you do, you will not be able to understand it is not mine.

245
00:29:03,617 --> 00:29:07,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and then the real homelessness is described as seeing the five aggregates as not yours.

246
00:29:08,585 --> 00:29:09,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, right.

247
00:29:09,947 --> 00:29:11,249
[SPEAKER_00]: I think there was a sort of...

248
00:29:24,929 --> 00:29:40,519
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why it says, whether it's past, present, future, inferior, sublime, coarse or subtle, all form is equally seen as this is not mine, I am not this, this is not myself.

249
00:29:41,480 --> 00:29:53,007
[SPEAKER_05]: So, rather than starting from, again, the standard attitude of management, which is like, I first get the form I want, and then I tell myself that it's not mine,

250
00:29:54,173 --> 00:29:58,956
[SPEAKER_05]: When it's, like, comfortable and easy, eh... no, it's the other way around.

251
00:29:59,676 --> 00:30:12,224
[SPEAKER_05]: You need to take on the uncertainty that any form might arise, any feeling might arise, any perception might arise, because you're not acting out of your gratuitous sense of control anymore.

252
00:30:12,264 --> 00:30:21,190
[SPEAKER_00]: So, factually, anything might happen, and that's how you would understand the true uncertainty, the aggregates, which would then free you from

253
00:30:22,583 --> 00:30:27,528
[SPEAKER_05]: Any suffering that would come on account of them, but at... not for free.

254
00:30:28,329 --> 00:30:32,813
[SPEAKER_05]: Not without the price of giving up the satisfaction of control.

255
00:30:57,452 --> 00:31:00,697
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the fear would also be gone, because now it doesn't matter what the advocates do.

256
00:31:01,178 --> 00:31:01,398
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

257
00:31:01,498 --> 00:31:02,479
[SPEAKER_01]: It's no longer a concern.

258
00:31:02,499 --> 00:31:12,775
[SPEAKER_05]: Then you realize, the fear wasn't, same as it was with all the other dharmas, the fear wasn't because of the external things, that, like, I didn't cultivate this kind of...

259
00:31:14,198 --> 00:31:16,722
[SPEAKER_05]: manly attitude of bravery or something.

260
00:31:16,762 --> 00:31:29,340
[SPEAKER_04]: That's always gonna be a way of just managing the aggregates such that they don't become a certain way when you perceive whatever.

261
00:31:30,301 --> 00:31:32,143
[SPEAKER_04]: Whereas, that's not taking you beyond fear.

262
00:31:32,683 --> 00:31:34,325
[SPEAKER_04]: That's like...

263
00:31:34,365 --> 00:31:40,910
[SPEAKER_04]: Same as when you look at, like, a certain food and you try to convince yourself that it's not tasty.

264
00:31:41,510 --> 00:31:44,092
[SPEAKER_04]: As if the lust was the fact that the food was tasty.

265
00:31:44,152 --> 00:31:44,453
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no.

266
00:31:44,493 --> 00:31:49,136
[SPEAKER_04]: The lust is, when food is tasty, you are lustful towards it.

267
00:31:49,156 --> 00:31:51,198
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what you have to train yourself in regard to.

268
00:31:51,258 --> 00:31:52,339
[SPEAKER_04]: The same as we said before.

269
00:31:52,979 --> 00:31:54,520
[SPEAKER_04]: You have to climb the hill.

270
00:31:54,540 --> 00:31:55,901
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, you have to run when it's uphill.

271
00:31:56,842 --> 00:32:10,220
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's when the fear is there, when the pressure is there, freeing yourself from that, from any need to act out of that, from any need to manage that and run away from that, that's the true going beyond fear.

272
00:32:10,801 --> 00:32:11,562
[SPEAKER_04]: Not like just...

273
00:32:12,123 --> 00:32:17,247
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, yeah, how many things are there in the world that you could be afraid of?

274
00:32:17,687 --> 00:32:18,048
[SPEAKER_04]: Infinite.

275
00:32:18,348 --> 00:32:18,548
[SPEAKER_04]: Yes.

276
00:32:18,868 --> 00:32:30,137
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you would never be able to just overcome fear by going through the specific things and toughening up until you can bear all of them.

277
00:32:31,337 --> 00:32:33,638
[SPEAKER_05]: Same with sensuality, same with aversion.

278
00:32:33,778 --> 00:32:42,660
[SPEAKER_05]: You cannot reach metta, like the boundless compassion that the Buddha talked about, by just spreading love towards each individual person or group.

279
00:32:43,100 --> 00:32:46,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, hoping that they don't become your enemies, so that you then feel at ease.

280
00:32:46,821 --> 00:32:52,862
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, see, it's... again, how many beings exist?

281
00:32:53,022 --> 00:32:56,403
[SPEAKER_04]: How many groups of beings exist that you're not even aware of?

282
00:32:57,043 --> 00:32:59,844
[SPEAKER_04]: So, the fact that you say, may all beings be happy, well...

283
00:33:01,064 --> 00:33:03,565
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't know what all is.

284
00:33:03,805 --> 00:33:08,106
[SPEAKER_04]: So, through that attitude, you will never be able to include everything.

285
00:33:09,446 --> 00:33:11,266
[SPEAKER_04]: Instead, you need to take it on the level of...

286
00:33:12,107 --> 00:33:16,988
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter how any being makes me feel internally.

287
00:33:18,128 --> 00:33:19,388
[SPEAKER_05]: Same as with the fear.

288
00:33:19,628 --> 00:33:24,009
[SPEAKER_04]: It doesn't matter what any external thing arises in these aggregates.

289
00:33:24,249 --> 00:33:25,910
[SPEAKER_04]: Because you dwell disconnected from the feeling.

290
00:33:26,030 --> 00:33:26,270
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

291
00:33:26,550 --> 00:33:27,050
[SPEAKER_04]: I will not...

292
00:33:27,130 --> 00:33:27,630
[SPEAKER_04]: I will not...

293
00:33:28,130 --> 00:33:31,051
[SPEAKER_05]: maintain the assumption of ownership in regard to it.

294
00:33:31,211 --> 00:33:39,793
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's why somebody who gives up appropriation of the five aggregates would have automatically developed metta, even if they never heard about that word.

295
00:33:40,093 --> 00:33:40,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

296
00:33:41,794 --> 00:33:50,496
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is why we can also perhaps reformulate saying that the right toughening up would be, as you said in the beginning, to start with the virtue.

297
00:33:50,676 --> 00:33:51,156
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, of course.

298
00:33:51,256 --> 00:33:53,157
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

299
00:33:53,297 --> 00:33:55,077
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're not keeping the precepts, but you're

300
00:33:57,866 --> 00:34:04,212
[SPEAKER_00]: I think we said that before, but you're putting up with all sorts of insane physical challenges.

301
00:34:05,454 --> 00:34:07,295
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not the right type of development, because...

302
00:34:07,896 --> 00:34:09,578
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, just think about it.

303
00:34:09,718 --> 00:34:20,048
[SPEAKER_05]: How many people are there in the world who can do difficult things on that physical level, compared to people who are celibate, by choice?

304
00:34:22,817 --> 00:34:23,177
[SPEAKER_05]: Forever.

305
00:34:23,917 --> 00:34:24,137
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

306
00:34:24,998 --> 00:34:37,462
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's, it's, it's... even if it seems like, you know, when people would say the monk's life is just, just a bunch of, bunch of good for nothing.

307
00:34:40,516 --> 00:34:46,619
[SPEAKER_04]: When you sit there, staring at the wall, you realize there's a reason why people don't do it.

308
00:34:46,919 --> 00:34:59,145
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's plenty of people in the world who just want to get easy food, easy lodgings, easy medicine, and they don't become monks.

